---
import Layout from '~/components/Layout.astro';
import { loadDataset } from '~/lib/data.ts';

const dataset = await loadDataset();
const counts = dataset.fellowships.map((f) => ({ ...f, n: dataset.meetings.filter((m) => m.f === f.code).length }));
Astro.response.headers.set('Cache-Control', 'public, max-age=300');
---

<Layout title="about double winners" index fellowships={dataset.fellowships}>
  <div class="layout">
    <div class="side">
      <h1><a href="/">double winners</a><small>about</small></h1>
      <div class="clock">a next-meeting finder for people in more than one program</div>
      <nav class="navline"><a href="/">all meetings</a> · <a href="/saved">saved</a> · <a href="/account">account</a></nav>
    </div>
    <div class="main prose">
      <h2>what this is</h2>
      <p>
        A "double winner" is someone in two fellowships. Most meeting directories are one-fellowship affairs. This one answers a
        smaller question across all of yours at once: what can I still make, right now or next?
      </p>
      <h2>anonymity</h2>
      <p>
        You never sign up. The first time you tap a fellowship or save a meeting, this site creates a random id and hands your browser a
        cookie for it. Your fellowships, timezone, saved and hidden meetings hang off that id and nothing else: no name, no email, no IP
        address, no browser fingerprint. Joining a meeting is a plain link that carries no referrer; no click is ever recorded. There are
        no analytics cookies and nothing loads from third parties. <a href="/account">Delete everything</a> in one tap, whenever.
      </p>
      <p>
        Plainly: the person running this site can technically read the database. What they would see is a list of random ids, each
        with a few preferences and a few meeting ids. Accounts that go quiet for six months are purged automatically.
      </p>
      <h2>where the meetings come from</h2>
      <p>
        Each fellowship's own virtual directory, re-read on a schedule. Meetings drift: links die, times move, groups fold. The
        fellowship's listing is always the source of truth, and each card links back to it where one exists.
      </p>
      <ul>
        {counts.map((f) => (
          <li>
            <a href={f.website ?? '#'} rel="noopener noreferrer">{f.name}</a> ({f.code}) — {f.n} weekly meetings
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p>Dataset {dataset.hash}, generated {dataset.generated.slice(0, 10)}.</p>
      <h2>fixing something</h2>
      <p>
        Wrong time, dead link, missing meeting? For now, correct it with the fellowship first (they own the listing) and it will flow
        here on the next pass. A report-a-problem button is coming.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
